## 5. Validation & Quality Gates

All tracks share these gates; a track is not "complete" until it passes them:

1. **Compile & unit test parity** — full build green, no suppressed warnings introduced by the migration.
2. **CCC closure check** — every CCC finding cited in Phase 0 as an acceptance criterion must show as remediated; re-query CCC, don't self-attest.
3. **Security regression pass** — auth/authz test suite (existing + Track A3 additions) green.
4. **Behavioral equivalence (Track B/C only)** — Litmus record-replay diff report attached with zero unexplained divergences, or explicitly waived divergences documented with rationale.
5. **AgentGuard dependency-graph check (if enrolled)** — confirm the PR's dependency graph delta matches the declared migration scope; unexplained new edges block merge.
6. **Rollback rehearsal** — for Track B extractions specifically, confirm the strangler-fig routing can be reverted to monolith-only with a config flip, not a code revert, before the extraction is considered production-ready.

If any gate fails, the agent returns to the relevant phase — it does not proceed to the next track or mark the Migration Plan complete.

---

## 6. Output Format & Deliverables

Every run of this skill produces, in this order:

1. **Migration Plan** (Markdown) — tracks selected, execution order, blast radius, risk tier, CCC/TABR constraints in force.
2. **Per-phase diff summaries** — file counts and risk flags per phase (per the token economy rule in §3.2), full diffs available on request.
3. **Validation Evidence Report** — results of every gate in §5, including the Litmus divergence report where applicable.
4. **Open Items / Human Decision Log** — anything the agent could not resolve autonomously (TABR conflicts, ambiguous bounded-context boundaries, distributed-transaction approvals needed) — surfaced explicitly, never silently assumed.

All four deliverables are written back to the target repo under `/.codemind/migrations/<run-id>/` and indexed via graphifyy so future runs (e.g., Track B after Track A) can reference prior decisions instead of re-deriving them.
